WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.823
"If you cross your eyes,
they'll stay that way."

00:00:02.823 --> 00:00:05.010
Man, that's an oldie but a goodie.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.510
"It's OK to go swimming

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.701
or take a shower in contact lenses."

00:00:08.701 --> 00:00:10.560
All right, everybody does it.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.820
But they really shouldn't.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.870
"Styes are contagious."

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:15.703
No!

00:00:15.703 --> 00:00:16.536
Not at all.

00:00:16.536 --> 00:00:17.840
Styes are not contagious.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.130
And you don't get them from pools.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:22.010
And you don't get them
from rubbing your eyes.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:22.843
Rupa Wong: Doorknobs.

00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:23.676
Jenifer Bossert: Kissing others.

00:00:23.676 --> 00:00:26.200
No, you don't get them
from any of those things.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.450
Aloha, my name is Dr. Jenifer Bossert.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:32.290
I am the optometrist at
the Honolulu Eye Clinic.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.210
I've been in practice for 30 years,

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:38.180
and my specialty is contact lenses.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.200
And aloha, everyone.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.470
I am Dr. Rupa Wong.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.780
I am a board-certified ophthalmologist.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.560
I've been in private practice
here in Hawaii for 13 years

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.380
working alongside this
wonderful lady here.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.400
I specialize in pediatric ophthalmology

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.700
and adult strabismus.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.300
And today we are here to
debunk myths about vision.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.840
We're going to start off with

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.700
debunking myths that we
used to think were true.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.230
"Reading in the dark or while lying down

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.540
will damage your vision."

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.950
No, this is a myth.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.040
It does not damage your vision.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.870
And my oldest son reads
in the dark all the time.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.100
My mother, obviously knows
I'm an ophthalmologist,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.170
still comes to my house and tells me,

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:20.420
"Your son should not read in the dark."

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.253
Not true.

00:01:21.253 --> 00:01:25.736
Because people need good
light to see better,

00:01:25.736 --> 00:01:29.050
they assumed maybe that when
you're reading in the dark,

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.920
because it is usually more challenging,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.700
maybe they thought that
was straining your eyes.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.870
People confuse those issues
with damaging your eyes.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.750
"Eating carrots will
improve your eyesight."

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.690
I have actually just studied

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.470
where the origin of this myth came from.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:46.868
It's really interesting.

00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:51.320
So, it was basically a
campaign during World War II

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.722
because the British air fighter pilots

00:01:54.722 --> 00:01:56.521
had this radar technology

00:01:56.521 --> 00:01:59.737
to be able to detect the German targets.

00:01:59.737 --> 00:02:01.580
But they didn't want the Germans to know

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.070
that they had the radar technology,

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.947
so they just said that their air pilots

00:02:05.947 --> 00:02:07.063
were eating a lot of carrots

00:02:07.063 --> 00:02:09.587
and therefore had good night vision.

00:02:09.587 --> 00:02:10.660
How fascinating!

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.010
See? I learned something today.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:12.843
I love it!

00:02:12.843 --> 00:02:15.680
Wong: Vitamin A is very
important for the metabolism

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.820
that's being performed in your retina.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.853
Bossert: A, C, E, magnesium,

00:02:20.853 --> 00:02:23.478
lutein, omega-3s,

00:02:23.478 --> 00:02:27.610
those are the common ones
that we all consider important

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.510
for optimum eye health.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:30.343
Zinc.

00:02:30.343 --> 00:02:31.234
Zinc.

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:34.252
Wong: All of these vitamins can help halt

00:02:34.252 --> 00:02:36.468
the progression of macular degeneration,

00:02:36.468 --> 00:02:38.634
but in the studies it didn't demonstrate

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:40.890
any more effect for people like us,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.020
that don't have any macular degeneration.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:46.630
"Wearing someone else's
glasses will ruin your vision."

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:49.490
If a child, really younger than 13,

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.590
is wearing someone else's glasses,

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.550
it can ruin their vision
if it is completely off.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.150
Because if it's promoting blurry vision,

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.660
that's going to inhibit the growth

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.890
of the connections between
the eyes and the brain.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.164
So kids under 13, they
are in a special period

00:03:08.164 --> 00:03:10.670
of their vision development
called the critical period.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.540
So, absolutely, I never, ever recommend

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.850
that children wear anybody else's glasses.

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.330
But for adults, it's a little
bit of a different story.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.000
Bossert: With adults, it
isn't going to harm your eyes,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.175
but it can contribute to eye fatigue,

00:03:25.175 --> 00:03:27.310
eye strain, headaches.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:28.950
So, yes, we always encourage everybody

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.320
to get their annual checkups,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.953
wear their own glasses,

00:03:31.953 --> 00:03:34.202
and keep them updated.

00:03:34.202 --> 00:03:38.040
"Sitting too close to the TV
is bad for your eyesight."

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.629
Typically a parent wants their child

00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:42.246
to move back from the television,

00:03:42.246 --> 00:03:43.662
and it's a total myth.

00:03:43.662 --> 00:03:46.480
And it doesn't harm an
adult's eyes, either.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.360
So, when I have a parent
ask me that in the exam,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:50.743
I encourage them to bring them in

00:03:50.743 --> 00:03:52.893
so that we can actually check their child

00:03:52.893 --> 00:03:55.328
and just see if it's a
habit that the child has

00:03:55.328 --> 00:03:56.510
or whether they actually have

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:00.090
an underlying nearsighted
process occurring.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.900
If they're sitting too
close to the television,

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.120
then I'm concerned that maybe they've been

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.090
developing nearsightedness.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.890
A young child doesn't know
that that's happening.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.470
Just because your parents are nearsighted

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.630
does not mean 100% you're
going to be nearsighted.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.410
Several studies have shown

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.667
that two hours a day
of sunlight is helpful

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:21.050
at preventing nearsightedness progression.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.300
So I always tell my
patients to get outdoors,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.530
but it's not so easy in other
parts of the country or world.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.210
"If you cross your eyes,
they'll stay that way."

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.980
Man, that's an oldie
but a goodie, I think.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.200
I remember my grandmother telling me.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.640
We were out running around,
and kids all playing,

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:38.610
and my grandmother saying,

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.280
"Your eyes are going to stay that way!"

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.330
Of course, that is actually

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.440
my area of specialty, is crossed eyes.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.530
That's what I do surgery for, to fix them.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.610
And I can tell you, I've never
had to operate on anybody

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:51.850
who crossed their eyes in intentionally

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:52.940
and it got stuck that way.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.420
So that's a complete myth.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.920
Some people are born with it.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.240
We call that congenital esotropia.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:59.480
Typically, if you're born with it,

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:00.950
you're born with crossed eyes.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:04.650
Sometimes people develop
it because the eye is blind

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.450
for whatever reason, a separate reason.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:07.750
So if they're young, then the eye

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:09.830
tends to cross in with the blind eye.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:13.010
If they're older and they
sustain some kind of trauma

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.580
or injury to the eye to
cause it to become blind,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:16.940
then the eye wanders out.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:19.420
'Cause I've seen so many patients

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.661
that have come from other eye doctors

00:05:21.661 --> 00:05:23.820
who have been told for years,

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.890
No. 1, "Your insurance doesn't cover it."

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.670
No. 2, "You're too old for this surgery."

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.800
I've operated on a 95-year-old.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.420
"Squinting is bad for your eyes."

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.895
We do know that when you squint,

00:05:35.895 --> 00:05:38.986
you tend to be able to
see a little bit better

00:05:38.986 --> 00:05:40.900
when you're nearsighted.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.270
Something called the pinhole effect.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.350
So that could be how
that myth got started.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.130
A parent might see their child squinting

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:49.550
and then think that it was the squinting

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.140
that actually caused the nearsightedness,

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.920
but it was the opposite.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.420
The nearsightedness caused the squinting.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.560
"You will become dependent on your glasses

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.240
if you wear them too much."

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.770
As someone who now has started
to need reading glasses,

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:09.770
it is really hard to
not believe this myth.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.290
I, when I take my reading glasses off,

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:16.090
I swear I could see the iPhone way better.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:19.060
Bossert: It's not that the
glasses made your vision worse,

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.840
it's just that your
brain got used to having

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.810
good, sharp vision when you put them on.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:25.900
So then when you take them off,

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.091
your brain's like,

00:06:27.091 --> 00:06:29.100
"No! I want them back again!

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.000
I like seeing clearly!"

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.933
"You can't wear your contacts

00:06:32.933 --> 00:06:34.860
if you have astigmatism."

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.400
This is definitely not true.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.460
I still hear that in this day and age,

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.259
despite media campaigns,

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:46.820
despite information dissemination.

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:50.210
People still believe that
if they have astigmatism,

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:52.480
they won't be able to wear contact lenses.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.290
Or that they're going to have
to be hard contact lenses.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:58.070
Yes. Because 30 years ago, it was true,

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.940
that really the only
way that you could mask

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.740
that astigmatism was to take a hard lens

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.080
and put it on the eye.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:09.540
But now, daily disposables, I can correct

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.740
2.75 units of astigmatism,

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.140
which is a lot.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:17.098
And in a reusable contact lens,

00:07:17.098 --> 00:07:20.700
I can go up to 5.75.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.341
I even have a brand-new option

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:24.140
that if you are over 40

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.840
and need reading glasses,

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.750
if you have astigmatism,

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:31.180
you can even wear a bifocal contact lens

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.080
that corrects for astigmatism now.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.570
But they are more difficult to fit

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:37.853
because they interact with the eyelid,

00:07:37.853 --> 00:07:39.610
the shape of the eye.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:41.418
So sometimes we'll have to go through

00:07:41.418 --> 00:07:43.401
two or three lens designs

00:07:43.401 --> 00:07:45.940
to find the best one for the patient.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:49.280
But it's pretty rare that
we can't find something

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:50.470
in this day and age.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:51.500
Wong: Now we're gonna talk about

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.450
myths we hear all the time.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:55.310
"Staring at a screen all day

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:57.650
will make your eyesight worse."

00:07:57.650 --> 00:07:59.408
Well,

00:07:59.408 --> 00:08:03.750
the jury's still a little
bit out on this one.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:07.620
Yes, anecdotally, we perceive that people

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.440
that spend longer hours on a screen

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.270
are the ones that are more
likely to end up nearsighted.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.150
But studies don't prove that.

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:17.660
And particularly with COVID,

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:21.040
with COVID and homeschooling,
online schooling,

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.160
we've seen a huge increase
in parents asking us,

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.250
"Do I need to get my kid blue blockers?"

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:29.560
Well, the answer is no.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:32.860
There was a small study
that came out and said that,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.150
yes, it could help decrease fatigue.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.610
But, like any study, we need
to take those small studies

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:41.860
and turn them into large studies

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.902
to really truly get facts and figures

00:08:45.902 --> 00:08:48.270
that hold out for the larger population.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.670
So when they redid the
study, it came out 50-50.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.470
It just really wasn't proven

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:56.200
to be statistically significant.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.750
Wong: And that's why we always
recommend the 20-20-20 rule.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:01.450
People need to take breaks

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:03.500
every 20 minutes for 20 seconds

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.290
where they look at something 20 feet away.

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:09.450
"Only people with bad
eyesight need eye exams."

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.750
That is definitely a myth.

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:15.220
There's all sorts of conditions
that still you have --

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.700
Retinal holes, retinal
tears, retinal detachments.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.910
That's another thing,
where people always think,

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.820
mistakenly, that they've had LASIK

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:29.040
and as if LASIK has
corrected their eyeballs.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:32.180
And it hasn't. It's just
made their vision better.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:36.170
But they still have the
pathology, the retinal issues,

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.850
that they still require exams once a year.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.760
Bossert: People think
that they're going to

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.570
the eye doctor for their vision.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.620
Eye doctors would tell
you that you're going

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.450
to the eye doctor for your eye health.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:51.090
Because if you don't
maintain the eye health,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.015
then you're not going to be able

00:09:53.015 --> 00:09:55.550
to maintain good eye vision

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:58.690
for the 100 years that
you're present on this earth.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:01.800
"You won't get glaucoma if
you have perfect vision."

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:05.440
Glaucoma is something that's asymptomatic.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:09.300
So you can have perfect 20-20 vision

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.680
and have the absolute
worst end-stage glaucoma.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.810
Glaucoma is typically high
pressure inside the eye,

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:19.190
which causes damage to the
optic nerve of the eye.

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:20.148
Bossert: And in the beginning,

00:10:20.148 --> 00:10:22.681
there are no signs whatsoever.

00:10:22.681 --> 00:10:25.640
So really the only way
that we can detect glaucoma

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.340
is to come in for your
annual eye examination.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.570
And there are a series of tests that we do

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:32.790
in the examination itself

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.800
that allow us to screen for glaucoma.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.820
So, when you catch glaucoma early,

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:39.910
then we can start treatments earlier.

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:43.700
And we can't reverse any vision loss

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.352
or any optic-nerve damage,

00:10:45.352 --> 00:10:48.490
but we can prevent future
damage from happening,

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.210
or at least slow that progression down.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:52.060
So, we start with eyedrops.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:53.870
We can even do laser treatments,

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.040
which help with that drainage system

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:57.930
and bring the fluid down.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:10:59.150
And then if we need to,

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:01.780
we escalate to glaucoma surgeries.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:03.680
Bossert: Myths from the internet.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:04.770
Let's do those next.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:06.260
"It's OK to go swimming

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:08.460
or take a shower in contact lenses."

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.143
All right, everybody does it.

00:11:11.143 --> 00:11:13.159
But they really shouldn't.

00:11:13.159 --> 00:11:15.730
You can really develop blinding infections

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:18.760
from tap water and from
water that's in hot tubs.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:20.610
Bossert: 'Cause no
matter what body of water

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.020
we're talking about,

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:26.720
they all have some bacteria
in them, even tap water.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.390
Which, that bacteria that bothers our eyes

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:30.720
doesn't bother our stomach,

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.610
yet you don't want to have it in the eye.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:39.620
Yes, do we all wash our
face and we get water in?

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:40.460
We do.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:42.276
But the critical thing is that

00:11:42.276 --> 00:11:44.584
this particular bug, called pseudomonas,

00:11:44.584 --> 00:11:47.710
is small enough that it can embed itself

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:50.740
in the pores, in the
matrix of the contact lens.

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:53.720
And then it gets on your
eye, and it sits there,

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:55.330
and it sits there, and it sits there,

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:57.760
for all those hours that you have it on.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.990
And that's when the risk becomes high.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:02.070
'Cause then you reach up and you rub,

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:06.170
you disturb the top layer
of cells on the eye.

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:08.170
Now there's a little
window for that bacteria

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:09.700
to walk right into the eye.

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:11.660
And unfortunately with pseudomonas,

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:13.880
we don't have good treatment modalities.

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:17.280
It's very resistant to the antibiotics

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.440
that we have available to us.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:22.310
"Stress causes eye floaters."

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:23.143
No.

00:12:23.143 --> 00:12:24.068
No.

00:12:24.068 --> 00:12:25.960
Age causes eye floaters.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:27.180
And trauma.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.910
A floater is just, it's
a vitreous detachment.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:32.610
And I tell my patients the
vitreous is like Jell-O.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:35.490
When you get older, it starts to liquefy,

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:37.170
and you get pockets, and dries up.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:39.460
And that's what causes separates

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:41.920
from the inner lining, from the retina.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:45.140
And that then floats all
around in your field of vision.

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.430
But it's actually inside your eye.

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.990
People think it's a cockroach,
they think it's an ant.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.630
They try to swat it away.

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.120
It's not any of those things,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:58.255
but it's an actual change
in the anatomy of your eye.

00:12:58.255 --> 00:13:00.090
It's not caused by stress.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:02.610
"LASIK surgery is not safe."

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:05.720
LASIK surgery is definitely safe.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:09.195
At this point, LASIK
surgery's been around ...

00:13:09.195 --> 00:13:12.870
35 years now?

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:16.511
Yes. If you were to do a Google search

00:13:16.511 --> 00:13:20.202
that pulled up some of
those early results,

00:13:20.202 --> 00:13:22.100
there were definitely instances

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.766
where there were

00:13:24.766 --> 00:13:27.470
cases of blindness.

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:30.970
A lot of that was changed

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:35.003
by further advancements in the technology,

00:13:35.003 --> 00:13:37.470
different ways to make the flap.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.550
So now, in this day and age,

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:43.080
I would say that LASIK
surgery is perfectly safe.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:47.440
That said, there is no
surgery for any body part

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:51.260
that doesn't come with
a risk of complications.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:53.310
Like, any body part.
Wong: Yeah, totally.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.770
You want the doctor
that's doing the procedure

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:56.920
on a weekly basis.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:59.110
You don't want the
doctor that's doing this

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.110
once every three or four months.

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:03.840
Wong: "Styes are contagious."

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:05.140
Bossert: No.
Wong: Not at all.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:06.760
Styes are not contagious.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.080
And you don't get them
from pools, public pools.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:12.740
Bossert: And you don't get
them from rubbing your eyes.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:14.820
Wong: Doorknobs.
Bossert: Kissing others.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:17.300
No, you don't get them
from any of those things.

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:20.130
Just like some people
can have more oily skin

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:21.710
or more dry skin.

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:23.520
I describe it kind of like a pimple.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.550
Bossert: The contents in the oil gland

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:28.000
can be different consistencies.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.500
So if you tend to have

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:33.633
that heavier, thicker oil

00:14:33.633 --> 00:14:36.280
inside the gland itself,

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:39.900
then you can be more
prone to getting them.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:42.400
Wong: And usually what
we want to do is heat.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.670
A lot of heat is going to help it drain.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:45.760
Pink eye is contagious.

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:47.670
What most people think of as pink eye

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:48.930
is viral conjunctivitis.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.590
Sometimes it can be
bacterial conjunctivitis.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.670
Viral conjunctivitis is
really, really contagious.

00:14:54.670 --> 00:14:58.828
That you do get from doorknobs
and the surface of objects.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:05.833
Is that something people think?

00:15:05.833 --> 00:15:07.080
Producer: Yeah.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:07.960
Not true.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:10.170
What I hope people take
away from this video

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:12.810
is that they get their annual eye exams,

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:16.600
they don't equal good
vision with eye health,

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.470
that they take their contact lenses out

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:20.460
when they sleep and they shower,

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:22.953
and that they follow the 20-20-20 rule.

